      "topic": "pk-punjab/xii/chemistry/c12-chem/ch18/18.10.2",
      "title": "18.10.2 Reaction of Manganate(VII) Ion with Iron(II) Ion",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "2d720c54-503b-58ec-b5b1-3307f222f40c",
          "pdf_from": 34,
          "pdf_to": 34,
          "note": "Topic 18.10.2, printed page 31."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This redox reaction involves the reduction of MnO₄⁻ and the oxidation of Fe²⁺ as indicated by the potential values. The relevant half-reactions (in reduction form) are:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{MnO4^{-} + 8H^{+} + 5e^{-} -> Mn^{2+} + 4H2O_{(l)}}\\qquad E^\\circ = +1.52\\ \\text{V}",
            "text_alt": "MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O(l)    E° = +1.52 V"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{Fe^{3+} + e^{-} -> Fe^{2+}}\\qquad E^\\circ = -0.77\\ \\text{V}",
            "text_alt": "Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺    E° = -0.77 V",
            "book_errors": [
              "E° = -0.77 V",
              "E^\\circ = -0.77"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "The book prints E° = −0.77 V for Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺; the standard value is +0.77 V, as the book's own Quick Check 18.8 gives."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Combine and balance the two equations and sum up their standard electrode potentials so that we can check feasibility of the overall reaction."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{MnO4^{-} + 8H^{+} + 5e^{-} -> Mn^{2+} + 4H2O_{(l)}}\\qquad E^\\circ = +1.52\\ \\text{V}",
            "text_alt": "MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O(l)    E° = +1.52 V"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "5 \\times [\\ce{Fe^{2+} -> Fe^{3+} + e^{-}}]\\qquad E^\\circ = +0.77\\ \\text{V}",
            "text_alt": "5 × [Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻]    E° = +0.77 V",
            "book_errors": [
              "E° = +0.77 V",
              "E^\\circ = +0.77"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{MnO4^{-} + 5Fe^{2+} -> Mn^{2+} + 5Fe^{2+} + 4H2O_{(l)}}\\qquad E^\\circ = +2.29\\ \\text{V}",
            "text_alt": "MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ + 4H₂O(l)    E° = +2.29 V",
            "book_errors": [
              "MnO₄⁻ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe²⁺",
              "E° = +2.29 V",
              "MnO4^{-} + 5Fe^{2+} -> Mn^{2+} + 5Fe^{2+}",
              "E^\\circ = +2.29"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "Correct working: written as an oxidation, 5 × [Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻] has E° = −0.77 V. The balanced overall equation is MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O (the book leaves out 8H⁺ and prints 5Fe²⁺ among the products). E°cell = 1.52 − 0.77 = +0.75 V, so the reaction is feasible."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The positive value of the standard electrode potential indicates that this reaction will proceed to the right, making this reaction feasible."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
